<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-linear-analysis" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Evaluation of Linear Models</title>
  <p> 
    This section is a continuation of the previous on linear
    regression.  Having defined what a linear regression is, I want to
    do some analysis of the quality of a regression. What is a good
    fit and how do we know? In this section, I will define three
    measures of the strength of a linear regression.
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term>correlation<term> of a linear model is a number
        <m>r</m>, with values between -1 and 1. A value of <m>r=1</m>
        indicates a perfect match with a line with positive slope. A
        value of <m>r=-1</m> indicates a perfect match with a line of
        negative slope. As the value of <m>r</m> gets closer to zero,
        it indicates a weaker and weaker match with the line. (In some
        context, this number is more specifically called the
        <term>Pearson correlation coefficient</term>.) 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    The term here is a bit annoying. We use <sq>correlation</sq>
    already to indicate a relationship, with positive correlation
    indicating that both variable grow goether and negative
    correlation indicating an inverse relationship. Now
    <sq>correlation</sq> is also a technical measure with the same
    name. This is frustrating, but it is standard terminology. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Let me use some diagrams to visualize correlation. What follows is
    a sequence of figure. The caption of each gives the correlation
    value, and you can see how the data becomes more scattered and
    less aligned to the linear model as the correlation gets close to
    zero. 
  </p>
