  <p>
    As a final note about correlation, this tool only measures how
    something fits. <xref ref="figure-correlation8"/> is one last
    correlation figure.  
  </p>
  <figure xml:id="figure-correlation8">
    <caption><m>r = 0.14</m></caption>
    <image xml:id="image-" width="80%">
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
          \begin{axis}[
            xlabel={Variable 1},
            ylabel={Variable 2},
            ]
            \addplot+ [only marks] table [
              x=var1,
              y=var2,
              col sep=comma,
              row sep=crcr
            ] {
              var1,var2\\
              2,2\\
              3,4\\
              5,17\\
              8,22\\
              11,35\\
              15,37\\
              16,37\\
              17,44\\
              21,49\\
              25,50\\
              32,42\\
              33,41\\
              34,31\\
              37,22\\
              41,20\\
              42,12\\
              49,11\\
            };
          \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>
  <p>
    This scatter plot has low positive correlation, r=0.14. There is a
    very slight positive correlation. However, the correlation
    entirely misses the fact that there is another kind of
    relationship here. A better model for this data would be a
    function that rises and then falls.  is not a linear model, so the
    correlation number doesn't see it at all. 
  </p>
  <p>
    In statistics, linear regression is almost always the first thing
    to try. But some data has relationship which are not linear. It's
    very valuable to remember that linear models are only one of many
    kind of models of relationship between data. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Now let me define the second measure of a linear model, which is
    very closely connected to correlation.
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The square of the correlation is simply called the <m>R^2</m>
        measure. (In some context, this is called the
        <term>coefficient of determination</term>. However, simply
        referring to it as <m>R^2</m> is so common that I'll stick
        with that convention. Also, the fact that we've change from a
        lower case <sq>r</sq> to an upper case <sq>R</sq> is annoying,
        but again is the conventional notation.) 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    By squaring the correlation, a couple things happen. First, the
    value of <m>R^2</m> is always positive, so it ignore the
    difference between positive and negative slopes. Close to 0 means
    that the data probably doesn't fit a linear model well, and close
    to 1 means a good fit. In this way, <m>R^2</m> is a slightly more
    elegant measure of linearity, and it gets used more often because
    of this. Second, by squaring, the numbers get slightly smaller.
    (If you square a number less than 1, it gets closer to zero).
    Therefore, <m>R^2</m> is a bit of a finer measure: <m>R^2 =
    0.9</m> is a stronger indication of a good linear fit than
    correlation of the same value. 
  </p>
  <p>
    In the previous section, I also talked multiple regression: making
    linear models with multiple explanatory variables. There are
    extension of <m>R^2</m> to multiple regression as well. The one
    which we will use in the classroom activities is called
    <term>adjusted <m>R^2</m></term>. You can assume that adjusted
    <m>R^2</m> means that we must be dealing with several explanatory
    variables. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Now let me get back to residuals, since these can also be used to
    evaluate the quality of a linear regression. Residuals were
    defined in the last section: they are the difference, for each
    data point, bewteen the actual data, <m>y</m>, and the prediction
    of the linear regression, <m>\hat{y}</m>. There is a residual for
    each data point: n in total, where n is the number of cases. 
  </p>
  <p>

Here is scatterplot from last video with the linear regression line 41
+ 0.59x. The residuals are the difference between the y coordinate of
the data point and the line. However, residuals are a bit tricky to
see in this form.

SLIDE

Instead, I can just plot the residuals themselves. Here is the same
data set, but the y axis is now the residual compared to the linear
regression. 0 is exactly on the line, this just shows how far above
and below the line each data point is. This is a residual plot. As a
visualization tool, the residual plot is extremely useful to seeing
how good the regression is, since all it shows is the error for each
data point. The shape and patterns of the residual plot can give
information.

In general, if there is not pattern to the residual plot (and the
residuals are not too large), that is good evidence that the original
regression was pretty good. For this data, from the last video, there
isn't much of a pattern to the residual plot, so the linear regression
is probably a decent model of the relationship between the variables. 

SLIDE

Here is another scatter plot. This looks like a pretty good
regression. What does its residual plot look like?

SLIDE

Looks pretty good. Balanced above and below the line, relatively small
residuals, no obvious pattern. This is evidence of a good linear
regression: a line that fits the data well.

SLIDE

Here is a very similar scatter plot. It also looks pretty good. What
does its residual plot look like?

SLIDE

This is a bit different. This residual plot has a bit of a pattern,
starting below, then moving above, then moving below. In the original
scatter plot, the variance of the data from the line was difficult to
read, visually. Here, the residual plot makes that much easier to
read. This is the strength of a residual plot: it zooms in on the
relationship between the data and the line, and can clearly show
things that were present in the original scatterplot, but much harder
to see. In this case, the relationship is still close to linear, but
this pattern means that there is probably something else going on here
as well, something non-linear between the two variables.

SLIDE

Lastly, residual plots can help understand the effect of outlier on a
linear regression. Here is a scatter plot with an outlier. This
outlier happens to be right on the linea regression line. 

SLIDE

Here is the matching residual plot. You can see the outlier over here.
The rest of the residual plot looks pretty good: no strong pattern,
data balances above and below the line. This outlier, though it may be
strange for other reasons, it no detrimentally affecting the
regression.

SLIDE

Here is another scatter plot, with an outlier well away from the
linear regression. The rest of this looks like a good fit, but it's
hard to read on the scatterplot.

SLIDE

Here is the residual plot. This makes it a bit more clear that the
outlier is having an effect. If the outlier is removed, more of these
points are below the line, and farther below the line. The outlier is
essentially pulling the line up just by itself. This can happen in
linear regression. 

At this point, a researcher would have to make a judgement about this
outlier. It is naturally part of the data? Or is it an error, which
could come from any number of sources (measurement error, data entry
error, bad experimental design that allowed in one eroneous data
point, and so on.) If there is such an argument, the the outlier
should be expluded and the line adjusted so that the residual plot
becomes more balanced. However, if the outlier does actually below,
then it means something and shouldn't be removed. As is the case so
often in this course, it comes down to judgement, experimental design,
and careful skepticism. 



